Q: Java CLI Task Tracker project A: <p>version 1.1</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public class TaskTracker {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Check if command is more than one string
        if (args.length &lt; 1) {
            System.out.println(&quot;Please enter a command such as: &lt;command&gt; &lt;id&gt; &lt;description&gt; &lt;status&gt;&quot;);
            return; // Removes the error message on empty input (What I Learned) Stops the main method from
            // trying to access args[0]
        }
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; args.length; i++) {
            System.out.println(&quot;[&quot; + i + &quot;] = &gt;&gt;&gt;&quot; + args[i] + &quot;&lt;&lt;&lt;&quot;);
        }
        // Store the command in a variable
        String command = args[0].toLowerCase(); // Handles case-sensitive situations from user input (What I Learned)
        System.out.println(&quot;command: &gt;&gt;&gt;&quot; + command + &quot;&lt;&lt;&lt;&quot;);
        // Switch statement
        switch (command) {
            case &quot;add&quot;:
                // FIXME
                System.out.println(&quot;Task added successfully (ID: 1)&quot;);
                break;
            case &quot;update&quot;:
                System.out.println(&quot;update&quot;); // FIXME
                break;
            case &quot;delete&quot;:
                System.out.println(&quot;delete&quot;); // FIXME
                break;
            case &quot;mark-in-progress&quot;:
                System.out.println(&quot;mark-in-progress&quot;); // FIXME
                break;
            case &quot;mark-done&quot;:
                System.out.println(&quot;mark-done&quot;); // FIXME
                break;
            case &quot;list&quot;:
                System.out.println(&quot;list&quot;); // FIXME
                break;
            case &quot;list done&quot;:
                System.out.println(&quot;list done&quot;); /* FIXME String with spaces are not being read from input */
                break;
            case &quot;list todo&quot;:
                System.out.println(&quot;list todo&quot;); // FIXME
                break;
            case &quot;list in-progress&quot;:
                System.out.println(&quot;list in-progress&quot;); // FIXME
                break;
            default:
                System.out.println(&quot;Please enter a valid command: &lt;command&gt; &lt;id&gt; &lt;description&gt; &lt;status&gt;&quot;);


        }


    }
}

</code></pre>
<p>Test Case: Input parameter <code>list todo</code></p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>$ java TaskTracker list todo
[0] = &gt;&gt;&gt;list&lt;&lt;&lt;
[1] = &gt;&gt;&gt;todo&lt;&lt;&lt;
command: &gt;&gt;&gt;list&lt;&lt;&lt;
list
</code></pre>
<p>Observation: You will find that the input &quot;list todo&quot; is parsed as two separate arguments:</p>
<ul>
<li>args[0] : <code>list</code></li>
<li>args[1] : <code>todo</code></li>
</ul>
<p>Consequently, your switch statement will never match case &quot;list todo&quot;:.</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Verify your input: Always use <code>System.out.println</code> to inspect the actual values of your input parameters.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Use unique log messages: Avoid using the same output string (e.g., &quot;TODO&quot;) in different cases. Using unique messages ensures you know exactly which logic path the program is executing.</p>
</li>
</ul>
