Q: How to check at compile-time if a = b (where a and b are arguments of type T&amp;) is valid inside a template function? A: <blockquote>
<p>I’m looking for a method that <strong>doesn’t require actually performing the assignment</strong>, ideally a trait or concept-based solution. <em>Is there a way in standard c++ to detect at compile-time whether a = b will compile</em> for a given type T inside a function template?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes, with C++20, you can use <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/requires.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>requires</code></a> and write a <code>concept</code> to check the validity of the expression <code>x= y</code> without actually performing the assignment as you require(pun intended) as shown below. Note that we also need to check <code>is_copy_constructible_v</code> in addition to <code>a=b</code> because of a <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/18405291/12002570">design flaw in <code>std::vector</code></a>. Here is the working example:</p>
<pre><code>
template&lt; typename T&gt; constexpr bool CAN_BE_COPIED = requires(T&amp;a, const T&amp;b)
{
 a = b;
 requires std::is_copy_assignable_v&lt;T&gt;;
 requires std::is_copy_constructible_v&lt;T&gt;;
};
//specialization for container types 
template&lt;typename T, template &lt;typename...&gt; typename  Container&gt;  constexpr bool CAN_BE_COPIED&lt;Container&lt;T&gt;&gt; = requires(std::vector&lt;T&gt;&amp;a, const std::vector&lt;T&gt;&amp;b)
{
 a = b;
 requires CAN_BE_COPIED&lt;T&gt;;
 requires std::is_copy_assignable_v&lt;T&gt;;
 requires std::is_copy_constructible_v&lt;T&gt;;
};
template&lt;typename T&gt; concept IS_COPY_ASSIGNABLE = CAN_BE_COPIED&lt;T&gt;; 


//DRIVER CODE TO VERIFY IT WORKS
int main() {
    using X = std::vector&lt;std::vector&lt;std::unique_ptr&lt;int&gt;&gt;&gt;;
    X v1, v2;
    foo&lt;X&gt;(v1, v2); // prints not possible 

    using Y = std::vector&lt;int&gt;;
    Y v3, v4;
    foo&lt;Y&gt;(v3,v4);   //prints possible

    int x{}, y{10};
    foo&lt;int&gt;(x,y);  //prints possible

    using Z = std::vector&lt;std::unique_ptr&lt;int&gt;&gt;;
    Z p, q;
    foo&lt;Z&gt;(p, q); // prints not possible

}
</code></pre>
